WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.860
- [Narrator] It's not just you

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:05.240
your daily coffee ritual
is getting more expensive.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.860
If you walk into an
American cafe right now

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:12.060
your average coffee drink is
going to run you around $4.50.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.023
Five years ago, it would
have cost you just $3.85.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.760
Nearly a 17% rise in prices.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.880
That's compared to
inflation, which has grown

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.200
around 10% during the same period.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.810
That rise is driven by a
myriad of economic forces

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.560
that help determine the
cost of your morning cup.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:33.660
And now experts say that due
to a major crop loss in 2020

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.820
in a matter of months,
prices could go up even more.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:37.743
We'll explain.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.400
The price of coffee is driven in part

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.410
by a complex global supply chain

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:47.890
which shapes a more than
$460 billion market.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:49.400
Most coffee beans are produced

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.800
in Brazil, Vietnam, and Columbia.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.930
They're then loaded on ships like these

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:56.170
and then transported around the world.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:58.460
This well-oiled system moves the beans

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:01.640
to importing countries like
the US, where they're then sold

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.220
to coffee traders who
sell them to roasteries.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.720
The roasted coffee is
then sold to distributors

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.230
such as grocery stores and cafes.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:11.890
Max Copestake is a Coffee Futures Broker.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.020
- The trade operates on paper-thin margin

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:16.270
it's extremely competitive business now.

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.000
Things like shipping costs are massive.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.060
- [Narrator] During the pandemic,

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.940
the industry has faced a
number of shipping disruptions.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.690
A shipping container
shortage in Southeast Asia

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:26.640
has driven up the price

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.100
of many commodities, including coffee.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.830
- Everything is taking longer to get

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:34.280
from one place to the other,

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:39.150
containers are piling up in
places where they shouldn't be.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:42.270
- [Narrator] Jose Sette is the
Executive Director of the ICO

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.230
a leading inter-governmental
organization for coffee.

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:48.490
- It's not just Southeast
Asia, all over the world

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:52.550
we have problems finding
sufficient containers.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:56.870
So that means that
freight rates tend to rise

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.850
and increase the prices.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:01.730
- [Narrator] Coffee exports
are also affected by storms.

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.580
In Nicaragua and Honduras
communities were devastated

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.130
by hurricanes last year.

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:08.890
- They caused a lot of
damage to infrastructure,

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:13.300
washed out bridges,
landslides that block roads.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.900
- [Narrator] The destroyed
roads block trade routes.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.760
And this meant the supply
being imported into countries

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.460
like the US was diminished.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.760
The concern over a lack of
supply help drive up the price

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.720
of coffee futures and experts
say this could continue.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.230
- As temperature increases

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:34.760
this affects the viability
of growing coffee

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.680
especially Arabica coffee.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:40.810
Robusta as its name indicates
is robust, is strong

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:45.800
and is harder to be affected
by climatic phenomenon.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:47.350
It's very resistant.

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:51.240
But Arabica coffee is more delicate.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.740
It grows within quite a
narrow temperature range.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.030
- [Narrator] Over the past year,

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.660
the world's largest
coffee producer Brazil,

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:02.220
faced a drought that devastated crops.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:05.790
The International Coffee
Organization says that around 30%

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:09.060
of the country's Arabica
crop may have been lost.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.310
This year's coffee
yields will also be lower

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.680
because Brazil is in an off-cycle harvest

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.750
which happens every other year.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:18.100
- The off-cycle is small,
and we know that anyway

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.160
but this year is extra small.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.070
And this has got people really spooked

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:23.417
'cause all of a sudden everyone's like,

00:03:23.417 --> 00:03:24.250
"Hang on.

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:25.900
We need this huge country

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.760
because we've become so reliant on you."

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.280
- [Narrator] Marex Spectron estimates

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:31.750
that the global coffee market

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:33.500
will produce millions fewer bags

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:35.160
than the previous crop year.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.320
You can see these factors are pushing

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.380
up the price of coffee
futures, but overall

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.500
the effects of the drought
haven't yet been felt

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.060
in your local cafe or grocery store.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.520
With weather becoming more
extreme around the world

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.710
due to climate change experts say risk

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.810
to coffee production will continue.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.400
- As temperatures increase,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.990
you would expect to find that areas

00:03:56.990 --> 00:04:01.990
that currently produce
Arabica will become too hot.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:07.540
And we have to think about
ways to mitigate this,

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:12.530
whether by different farming
techniques or whether

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:17.520
and this is a more difficult
process, migrating production.

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:19.980
- [Narrator] And as volatile
weather increasingly leads

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:22.210
to fluctuations in the supply of coffee,

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:24.370
demand tends to stay pretty steady.

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:27.140
This is because coffee
is relatively inelastic,

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:29.510
meaning that even when
the economy fluctuates,

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:31.480
people keep buying beans.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:36.373
- It doesn't change much
if prices go up or down.

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:41.430
Let me try and explain
this in simple terms.

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:46.410
If prices double in your
supermarket next week,

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:51.410
you are likely to change
your habits to some extent.

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:56.463
You will maybe switch
over to a cheaper brand.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:02.930
You may not drink as much
coffee outside the home

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:05.570
where it is more expensive.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:08.620
- But it's unlikely you'll stop
drinking coffee altogether.

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:11.330
When the pandemic took
hold in March, 2020,

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.130
people were forced to stay home.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:15.300
Demand for out of home coffee plunged,

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.640
but demand for in-home coffee rose

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.290
making up some of the
difference in consumption.

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:23.000
And as the pandemic
continued, another pressure

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.350
on coffee pricing was playing
out in global currencies.

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:28.040
Coffee future prices are tied

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.420
to the US dollar and Brazilian real.

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:32.200
The dollar is a global safe haven

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:34.140
in times of economic trouble.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:37.660
So during the pandemic coffee
farmers were incentivized

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:39.950
to take advantage of
the strong exchange rate

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:41.540
and sell off their beans.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:45.330
The result less stored coffee
will be available next harvest

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:48.590
when crop yields are
expected to be unusually low,

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:50.300
this is causing an imbalance

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:52.220
between the lower supply of coffee

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:54.400
and the expected rise in demand.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.870
And this is why experts say coffee prices

00:05:56.870 --> 00:05:58.870
are currently lower than they should be.

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:01.490
They say that this expected rise in prices

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:03.390
will affect the entire supply chain.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.380
And in six to nine months

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:08.853
it could drive up the price of
your morning cup, even more.

00:06:09.775 --> 00:06:12.442
(upbeat music)

